From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oliver Sargison <sargo@sargo.cc>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +1300
Subject: [PATCH 07/14] Plutonium: the switcher (Super+Tab), desktop settings
 and display configuration

Ash's overlay that switches between shells, lists windows and installed shells, copies a prompt for the user's agent, opens the backup terminal, and holds the desktop settings and display configuration.

It holds these changes:
- tags and groups on windows and tabs, the Super+Tab overlay
- verlay: no empty Shells heading when a search matches no shell
- the switcher: shell pages, fullscreen windows, backup terminal and browser
- several displays, a shell per display, one window in one place
- the switcher's text fields are light on dark, its rows hover steadily, the backup window is in front
- the switcher's installed shells: an exact list from the launcher, customizing and server controls on a shell's page
- the switcher copies a prompt for the user's own agent instead of starting one; a New shell entry on its Add page
- app icons in the switcher's window list and launcher
- desktop settings and a display configuration tool in the switcher

and the parts in these files of:
- shell mode on DRM, evdev input fix, display-plane path
- several live shells, Super+Tab switches between them
- Google sign-in on the web; tabs fullscreen outside the shells
- downloads for the shells' tabs
- file pickers for tabs ask the shell
- screen capture for shells, capture requests to the focused shell
- the shells' tabs survive a restart
- the hardware keys go to the helper's desktop services; the browser's media session in the Plutonium domain
- the lock screen and idle: Ash's own lock over every display, Super+L, the idle time, the wake locks and the displays' power in the Plutonium domain
- touchpad swipes with three fingers or more go to the shell page
- xdg_toplevel_icon_v1, apps' window icons for the shells
- Super+F maximizes in the shell, Super+Shift+F is fullscreen; menus over a fullscreen element; a backdrop for presented windows
